Good question - let me think through a comprehensive CLI/config design for walsync watch.

Core concepts to clarify:

WAL sync - Incremental sync of WAL changes (fast, frequent)
Snapshots - Full database upload (slower, less frequent)
Retention - How long to keep old snapshots/WAL segments
Cleanup - When to delete expired data
Proposed config structure:


# walsync.toml

[storage]
bucket = "s3://my-bucket/backups"
endpoint = "https://fly.storage.tigris.dev"
region = "auto"

[sync]
interval = "1s"              # How often to check for WAL changes
batch_size = 100             # Max WAL frames per upload

[snapshots]
interval = "1h"              # How often to take snapshots
on_startup = true            # Take snapshot when watch starts

[retention]
snapshots = "30d"            # Keep snapshots for 30 days
wal = "7d"                   # Keep WAL segments for 7 days
# Or more granular:
# hourly = 24                # Keep 24 hourly snapshots
# daily = 30                 # Keep 30 daily snapshots  
# weekly = 12                # Keep 12 weekly snapshots

[cleanup]
interval = "1h"              # How often to run cleanup
dry_run = false              # Preview deletions without executing

[[databases]]
path = "/data/this.db"
prefix = "this"

[[databases]]
path = "/data/that.db"
prefix = "that"

[[databases]]
path = "/data/other.db"
prefix = "other"
snapshot_interval = "30m"    # Override per-database
CLI equivalent:


walsync watch this.db that.db other.db \
  --bucket s3://my-bucket \
  --sync-interval 1s \
  --snapshot-interval 1h \
  --retention 30d \
  --cleanup-interval 1h \
  --config walsync.toml
Other features to consider:

Feature	Description
Compression	--compress zstd - Compress snapshots/WAL before upload
Bandwidth limit	--max-bandwidth 10MB/s - Throttle uploads
Concurrent uploads	--max-concurrent 4 - Parallel upload limit
Health endpoint	--health-port 8080 - HTTP health check for k8s/nomad
Metrics	--metrics-port 9090 - Prometheus metrics endpoint
Webhooks	--webhook-url https://... - Notify on snapshot/error
Encryption	--encrypt-key $KEY - Client-side encryption
Verify schedule	--verify-interval 24h - Periodic integrity checks
Graceful shutdown	Handle SIGTERM, complete in-progress uploads
Resume state	Track progress in local state file
Snapshot trigger	SIGUSR1 triggers immediate snapshot
Tiered retention example:


[retention]
# Grandfather-father-son backup rotation
hourly_snapshots = 24    # Keep last 24 hours
daily_snapshots = 7      # Keep last 7 days (1 per day)
weekly_snapshots = 4     # Keep last 4 weeks (1 per week)
monthly_snapshots = 12   # Keep last 12 months (1 per month)
Status/monitoring commands:


# Show current sync status
walsync status --config walsync.toml

# List all snapshots with retention info
walsync list --bucket s3://my-bucket --show-retention

# Preview what cleanup would delete
walsync cleanup --bucket s3://my-bucket --dry-run

# Force immediate snapshot
walsync snapshot this.db --bucket s3://my-bucket

# Verify all backups
walsync verify --bucket s3://my-bucket
