Right - trigger-based sync is smarter than pure intervals. Options:

Sync triggers (any of):


[sync]
# Trigger sync when ANY condition is met:
max_changes = 100        # After N WAL frames
max_interval = "10s"     # After N seconds
max_bytes = "1MB"        # After N bytes accumulated
on_checkpoint = true     # When SQLite checkpoints the WAL
Snapshot triggers (any of):


[snapshots]
# Trigger snapshot when ANY condition is met:
interval = "1h"          # Every hour
on_wal_size = "100MB"    # When WAL exceeds size
on_changes = 10000       # After N total changes
on_idle = "5m"           # After 5 min of no writes
on_startup = true        # When watch starts
on_shutdown = true       # Graceful shutdown snapshot
Combined example:


[sync]
max_changes = 100
max_interval = "10s"
# → Sync after 100 changes OR 10 seconds, whichever first

[snapshots]
interval = "1h"
on_idle = "5m"
# → Snapshot every hour OR after 5 min idle, whichever first
CLI:


walsync watch app.db \
  --sync-changes 100 \
  --sync-interval 10s \
  --snapshot-interval 1h \
  --snapshot-on-idle 5m
Other trigger ideas:

Trigger	Use case
on_signal	SIGUSR1 forces immediate snapshot
on_cron	Cron expression (0 * * * *)
on_file	Touch a file to trigger
on_http	POST to health endpoint triggers snapshot
min_interval	Debounce - don't sync more often than X
The "whichever comes first" pattern with max_changes + max_interval is probably the sweet spot for WAL sync. Want me to update the docs with this design?

